hawk@slytherin.ds.psu.edu (Dr. Richard E. Hawkins) writes:

> It's been a few years, but isn't German grammar a subset of English
> grammar?

Bwa ha ha ha ha.  No.

Having actually done some software internationalization work, I would
have to say NetHack would be a nightmare to translate.  Since nearly all
the messages are embedded in the source code (except for things like
cookie fortunes) and a lot of messages are constructed on-the-fly with
underlying assumptions that really only hold for English grammar (like
being able to drop words into the middle of a sentence without having to
adjust anything else for case or number agreement), not to mention all
the special casing to deal with quirks of English usage, it would be one
incredibly long tedious slog.  Which you'd have to repeat as the code
mutates (a fair number of changes are things like fixing various
grammatical mistakes in messages or creating things like the You()
function that strips all the leading "You"s out of messages).

If someone did a Spanish version they already took on the nightmare but
it would be a new nightmare for each language you wanted to translate
NetHack into.  German would be particularly difficult due to the case
and gender marking; message substitutions would have to contain
appropriate agreement articles and nouns change their ending slightly in
different grammatical cases.  And you'd need a German to tell you which
nouns are which gender since it's not possible to figure it out just by
looking at the word.

